{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794360236443,"sessionID":"ses_e37d2cb66ffeKlDfh1f9c34oTg","part":{"id":"prt_1c8345985001fAxfWrkoKEMPEO","messageID":"msg_1c834064d001VosTNcVOxKqh5S","sessionID":"ses_e37d2cb66ffeKlDfh1f9c34oTg","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794360236444,"sessionID":"ses_e37d2cb66ffeKlDfh1f9c34oTg","part":{"id":"prt_1c8345988001RfXsZ1gCmp9Jy5","messageID":"msg_1c834064d001VosTNcVOxKqh5S","sessionID":"ses_e37d2cb66ffeKlDfh1f9c34oTg","type":"text","text":"<think>The lock file has hashes. The error says flask's downloaded archive hash was f4bce... which IS in the lock (line 11). Hmm, the error message shows `--hash=sha256:f4bce...` missing? It says \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some packages\". Wait, the message shows the hash it had: f4bce... which is in the lock as the second hash. Why the mismatch?\n\nAh — I see. When using `-c constraints.txt` (not `-r`), pip only treats the constraint file as constraints, not as a requirements file with hashes. But the error mentions `--require-hashes mode` — hmm, it says \"note that it enables automatically when any package has a hash\". The constraint file has hashes → pip enables require-hashes mode for the whole resolution, and the flask package's hashes are not present in the *requirements* file (requirements.txt has no hashes) → error.\n\nSo `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` with a hash-bearing constraints file triggers require-hashes mode. But the Dockerfile does exactly this! So the Docker build would fail?? Let me look at the Dockerfile again:\n\n```\nRUN if [ -f backend/requirements.lock ]; then \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock; \\\n```\n\nHmm, that would hit the same error. Unless... pip's behavior: when `-c` contains hashes, pip's require-hashes mode applies? The error above proves it fails in this environment's pip version (25.1.1).\n\nWait — maybe the Docker build uses a different pip version where this works. Python 3.13-slim image ships pip 25.x. Hmm.\n\nLet me test the exact Docker command to see if it fails. If it fails, the whole image build fails → the app won't start → catastrophic. But the scaffold was given as-is with this instruction, so presumably the Docker build works in their environment. The difference: my local pip is 25.1.1 from the system; the Docker image python:3.13-slim may have pip 25.0.x or newer.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: pip's require-hashes mode is enabled when any requirement (including constraints?) specifies hashes. In pip's implementation, `RequirementsFile` options include `require_hashes` per file; the constraint file's hashes set require_hashes=True for that file's options, but the global `--require-hashes` flag is computed as `any(options.require_hashes for options in ...)`. Hmm, in pip's `parse_requirements`, the hash-checking mode is enabled if the *requirements* file has hashes... Let me look at the actual error: it complains about flask==3.1.3 missing hashes, while flask comes from requirements.txt (no hashes) — so require-hashes mode is global.\n\nIndeed, pip's code: `self.require_hashes = all(...)`? Let me check pip source in this environment to understand. Actually, pip's `InstallationReport`... Let me look at pip/_internal/resolution/resolvelib/... Hmm, simpler: pip's `req_command`/`parse_requirements` → `RequirementsFile.parse` collects `options.require_hashes`; then `cmdline` sets `options.require_hashes = options.require_hashes or finder...`? \n\nIn pip/_internal/cli/req_command.py:\n```python\n@classmethod\ndef add_options(cls):\n    ...\ndef handle_pip_version_check...\n```\nAnd in `parse_requirements`:\n```python\nfor req_file in req_files:\n    ...\n```\nHmm. The relevant code in pip/_internal/req/req_file.py:\n\n```python\nclass RequirementsFile:\n    def __init__(self, path, constraint):\n        ...\n        self.options: Options = Options()\n        ...\n        if self.options.require_hashes:\n            ...\n```\nAnd in `pip/_internal/cli/req_command.py`:\n```python\ndef parse_requirements(self, filename, constraint, finder):\n    ...\n```\nAnd in `pip/_internal/commands/install.py`:\n```python\nreqs = self.get_requirements(args.constraints, args.editable, finder, options)\n```\nThe require-hashes global: in `pip/_internal/req/__init__.py`:\n```python\ndef get_requirements(...):\n```\nHmm, in pip/_internal/resolution/resolvelib/... The mode is set in `pip/_internal/commands/install.py::run`:\n```python\nif options.require_hashes ...\n```\nActually I recall: `pip/_internal/req/req_file.py` → `parse_requirements` → returns `ParsedRequirements` with `options.require_hashes`; in `pip/_internal/cli/req_command.py::get_requirements`:\n```python\nfor req_file in req_files:\n    parsed = ...\n    requirements.extend(parsed.requirements)\n    options = parsed.options\n    ...\n```\nHmm, there's this in `pip/_internal/commands/install.py`:\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def get_requirements(cls, args, finder):\n```\nWhatever. The empirical evidence: pip 25.1.1 fails with `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` where the lock has hashes.\n\nBut wait — the lock file was presumably generated by the task author with `pip-compile --generate-hashes`, and the Dockerfile installs with `-c`. If that fails in the Docker build too, the scaffold is broken. That seems unlikely — the task says \"The app is built from the Dockerfile in the workspace root and started from the resulting image\", so the build must work.\n\nLet me check pip's source to see whether constraint-file hashes trigger require-hashes mode.\n\nIn pip/_internal/req/req_file.py (pip 25.x):\n\n```python\n    def parse_requirements(self, finder):\n        ...\n```\nand \n\n```python\nclass ParsedRequirement:\n    ...\n```\n\nIn pip/_internal/cli/req_command.py:\n\n```python\n    def get_requirements(\n        self,\n        args: list[str],\n        constraint: bool,\n        finder: PackageFinder,\n    ) -> list[Requirement]:\n        ...\n        for req_file in args:\n            parsed = functools.partial(self._parse_file, ...)\n```\nHmm.\n\nThe key: `Options.require_hashes` is a per-file flag, and `pip/_internal/resolution/resolvelib/factory.py` checks `self._require_hashes`... Actually there's this in `pip/_internal/req/req_install.py`:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def _strip_extras(...)\n```\n\nLet me just check the installed pip source to be accurate.\n\nActually the empirical result is what matters most: the Docker build might use a pip version where this works. But since I can't run Docker here (maybe I can?), I should make the setup robust: I could regenerate/rewrite requirements.lock without hashes, or restructure so that the lock is installed directly as `-r backend/requirements.lock` (which contains everything needed with hashes). But the README says the lock \"holds the resolved versions and is installed as a pip constraints file\".\n\nSafest approach: keep requirements.lock as-is (hashes) but ensure the Docker install command works. Options:\n1. Modify the Dockerfile to `pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.lock` — but the lock's `# via -r requirements.txt` comments don't matter; it lists all transitive deps with hashes → require-hashes mode works because every package has hashes. This is the standard pip-compile output usage: `pip install -r requirements.txt` where requirements.txt has `-r requirements.lock`? Hmm, typically pip-compile generates requirements.txt with hashes and you install that.\n\n2. Modify the lock file to remove hashes (just pinned versions) → then `-c` works fine and matches the README description (\"holds the resolved versions and is installed as a pip constraints file\"). The README says \"Dependencies are pinned: backend/requirements.lock holds the resolved versions and is installed as a pip constraints file.\" That suggests the intended lock has resolved versions; hashes are optional.\n\nBut wait: the grader might rebuild the image using the given Dockerfile — if I modify the Dockerfile, that's allowed? The task says \"The app is built from the Dockerfile in the workspace root\" — I can modify it as long as it still builds and works. But careful: maybe the grading harness uses its own copy of the Dockerfile? Unlikely; it says the app is built from the Dockerfile in the workspace root.\n\nHmm, but is the current pip failure real in the Docker image? Let me check the pip version in python:3.13-slim. The image is pinned by digest. I can't pull it (no network except the package registry proxy?). Let me check if docker is available in this sandbox.\n\nLet me first check pip's behavior more concretely and check the pip version in the Docker base image. python:3.13-slim currently ships pip 25.x (e.g. 25.0.1 for 3.13-slim around mid-2025, maybe 25.2+ later). The require-hashes behavior in pip has been stable for years: \"pip install -r requirements.txt -c constraints.txt\" where constraints.txt has hashes → I believe pip enables require-hashes globally, causing this error. This has been a known issue: hash-checking mode is triggered if ANY file (including constraints) contains hashes.\n\nLet me verify by reading pip's source in this environment.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794360236424,"end":1794360236440}}}
