{"type":"text","timestamp":1794360301304,"sessionID":"ses_e37d2cb66ffeKlDfh1f9c34oTg","part":{"id":"prt_1c83556e5001EsGlFfU9X7KvBF","messageID":"msg_1c8354362001aplrNv7h2f4tPb","sessionID":"ses_e37d2cb66ffeKlDfh1f9c34oTg","type":"text","text":"<think>The lock has 8 packages: flask + gunicorn and their transitive deps (blinker, click, itsdangerous, jinja2, markupsafe, werkzeug).\n\nNow, the install with `-c` and hashes fails. Let me verify the exact Docker command fails, then decide.\n\nLet me test: create a venv and run `pip install -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock` → we already saw it fail with the system pip.\n\nDecision: rewrite requirements.lock as a hash-free constraints file with the same pins. This makes the documented Docker command work with any modern pip.\n\nLet me verify the fix works by installing into a venv.\n\nActually wait. Before rewriting the lock, let me consider: is it possible that the Docker environment's pip is configured with `PIP_NO_REQUIRE_VIRTUALENV` etc.? Irrelevant.\n\nAnother thought: maybe I can keep the hashes AND make the install work by adding `--no-deps`? With --no-deps, pip installs only the listed requirements (flask, gunicorn) and ignores dependencies → gunicorn needs packaging? Gunicorn 26 requires packaging? Actually gunicorn's deps: setuptools (for pkg_resources?) and packaging. With --no-deps, gunicorn might break at runtime if it imports packaging. Let me not.\n\nGo with stripping hashes. Let me write the new lock file.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794360301285,"end":1794360301301}}}
