{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794360491590,"sessionID":"ses_e37d2cb66ffeKlDfh1f9c34oTg","part":{"id":"prt_1c8383e3300186RdOzK5ZWD6W4","messageID":"msg_1c837f518001E8lE4wyOyfES61","sessionID":"ses_e37d2cb66ffeKlDfh1f9c34oTg","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794360491590,"sessionID":"ses_e37d2cb66ffeKlDfh1f9c34oTg","part":{"id":"prt_1c8383e35001sUwosG9mtbxLC9","messageID":"msg_1c837f518001E8lE4wyOyfES61","sessionID":"ses_e37d2cb66ffeKlDfh1f9c34oTg","type":"text","text":"<think>Tailwind utilities are compiled into the CSS.\n\nNow, I cannot run a browser to verify the UI. Let me at least do a DOM-level sanity test using a lightweight approach — maybe I can render the React app in Node with a fake DOM? That would require jsdom (installable from npm). That's a reasonable way to smoke-test the app: install jsdom as a dev dependency? That adds to the lock file and the Docker build (npm ci installs dev deps in the build stage — fine, but it bloats). I could install jsdom temporarily in a separate directory outside the frontend package to avoid polluting package.json.\n\nLet me do that: create /workspace/.uitest/ with package.json installing jsdom + react + react-dom from the frontend's node_modules... Hmm, simpler: use the frontend's node_modules (it has react/react-dom) and install jsdom into a temp dir with npm --prefix.\n\nPlan:\n1. `npm --prefix /workspace/.uitest install jsdom` (creates /workspace/.uitest/node_modules)\n2. Write a script that:\n   - creates a jsdom window with the built index.html\n   - imports the built JS bundle? The built bundle is an ES module referencing `/assets/...`. I can set up jsdom with `runScripts: \"dangerously\"` and resources. Loading ES modules in jsdom isn't supported (\"script type=module\" is not executed by jsdom).\n\nAlternative: build a test bundle with vite in library mode? Too complex.\n\nAlternative: use jsdom + esbuild to bundle the TSX source for the test (esbuild is available as a vite dependency in node_modules). I can run esbuild to bundle src/main.tsx into a CJS file, then run it in a jsdom environment, and interact with the DOM.\n\nThis is doable: node_modules/.bin/esbuild exists in frontend/node_modules (vite depends on esbuild? In Vite 8, rolldown/oxc may be used, but esbuild might still be present). Let me check.\n\nActually, Vite 8 uses Rolldown (oxc) — esbuild may not be installed. Let me check node_modules/.bin.\n\nAlternatively, I can write a small test using React's test renderer? Not installed either.\n\nHmm, is this worth it? A DOM smoke test would catch runtime errors (e.g., a bad import, a crash on render). The typecheck + build already catch import errors and type errors. Runtime errors like \"cannot read property of undefined\" are possible but I've reviewed the code.\n\nLet me at least do a lightweight check: use node to evaluate the bundle with a minimal DOM stub? The bundle calls `document.getElementById(\"root\")` and React's DOM renderer — needs a real DOM.\n\nLet me check if jsdom is installable and if esbuild/rolldown binaries are available for bundling the source.\n\nActually, simpler: vite can build a test bundle! I can run `vite build` with a custom config (SSR/lib mode) targeting node, producing a CJS bundle of src/main.tsx that requires a DOM global. I can set up jsdom globals then import the bundle.\n\nSteps:\n1. `npm --prefix /workspace/.uitest install jsdom` → gives jsdom.\n2. Use vite's own build API programmatically? Or use `npx vite build --config /workspace/.uitest/vite.test.config.ts` with `build.ssr: \"src/main.tsx\"` and `build.rollupOptions.output.format: \"cjs\"`. Vite 8 (rolldown) supports SSR builds.\n\nThen in the test script:\n- create jsdom, set global.window/document/etc.\n- require the built CJS bundle → it renders the App into #root.\n- Then simulate: fill the username/password inputs, click register, wait, and check the DOM updates.\n\nThe fetch calls need a mock: I can stub global.fetch to route to a fake API implementation (in-memory). That's a decent integration test of the frontend logic.\n\nThis is a decent investment to verify the UI works. Let me try it — but keep it time-boxed.\n\nLet me first check the availability of jsdom in the npm registry and whether vite SSR build works.\n\nActually, there's an even simpler approach for bundling: vite's `build.lib` mode with `ssr: true`. Let me write a config file in .uitest:\n\n```ts\nimport react from \"@vitejs/plugin-react\";\nimport { defineConfig } from \"vite\";\nexport default defineConfig({\n  root: \"/workspace/frontend\",\n  plugins: [react()],\n  build: {\n    ssr: true,\n    outDir: \"/workspace/.uitest/dist\",\n    emptyOutDir: true,\n    rollupOptions: { input: \"/workspace/frontend/src/main.tsx\", output: { format: \"cjs\", entryFileNames: \"app.cjs\" } },\n  },\n});\n```\n\nHmm, with `build.ssr: true` and rollupOptions.input as a file path. Vite might complain about `ssr` + `rollupOptions.input`. Let me just try.\n\nThen the test script (node, CommonJS):\n```js\nconst { JSDOM } = require(\"/workspace/.uitest/node_modules/jsdom\");\nconst dom = new JSDOM(`<!doctype html><html><body><div id=\"root\"></div></body></html>`, { url: \"http://localhost/\" });\nglobal.window = dom.window; global.document = dom.window.document; global.HTMLElement = dom.window.HTMLElement; ...\n```\n\nReact 19 uses `queueMicrotask`, `MessageChannel`, `requestAnimationFrame` — jsdom provides most; MessageChannel exists in Node's global but React checks `window.MessageChannel`. jsdom has MessageChannel? jsdom does implement MessageChannel. OK.\n\nThen `require(\"/workspace/.uitest/dist/app.cjs\")`.\n\nThen mock fetch:\n```js\nglobal.fetch = async (url, init) => { ... }\n```\nReturn a Response-like object with `ok`, `status`, `json()`, `headers`, `blob()`.\n\nI need `Headers` and `Response` globals — Node 18+ has them globally (undici). Since the bundle uses `new Headers(...)` → global Headers exists in Node 22+. And `response.json()` etc. are from my mock. But `Headers` must be the same realm? Fine.\n\nThen simulate interactions:\n- After render, wait a tick, then find the username input, set its value via the native setter, dispatch an \"input\" event (React 19 listens to \"input\" for onChange).\n- Click the Register tab, etc.\n\nThis is somewhat involved but let me try — it validates the frontend end-to-end logic.\n\nLet me check if jsdom installs quickly.</think>\n\nLet me set up a DOM smoke test for the frontend to verify runtime behavior:","time":{"start":1794360491573,"end":1794360491587}}}
